<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part IV &mdash; Torus and Möbius &middot; Chapter 7</p>
  <h1>The Inside of the Outside</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">The circle and the torus are levels in the chain: stable, closed, orientable. The
    Möbius strip is something else entirely &mdash; a transition between levels, the surface of the
    moment when what was outside one level becomes inside the next.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">7.1 &mdash; The complete vocabulary</p>
    <h2 id="t-four-objects">The Four Geometric Objects</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The circle</strong> &mdash; π applied once: a line curved into a closed loop.</li>
      <li><strong>The torus</strong> &mdash; π applied twice: a loop curved around another loop.</li>
      <li><strong>The complex plane</strong> &mdash; generated by π/2: the half-rotation that creates the
        imaginary axis.</li>
      <li><strong>The Möbius strip</strong> &mdash; π applied as a half-twist that
        <em>inverts orientation</em> rather than closing it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Three of these are places to be. The fourth is a way of getting from one to another, and that
      distinction is what the rest of the chapter is built on.</p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's argument, contested</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

